--- PROVENANCE NOTE, appended by the operator after the run ---

This log is TRUNCATED. The four RTT lines and every derived figure above are the
real, complete output of the probe; the trailing "wall clock end" / "load
average end" lines are missing because the surrounding shell wrapper hit a
9m50s harness timeout and was killed AFTER the measurement had printed but
BEFORE the wrapper's own footer ran. Nothing above this note was edited.

Observed end conditions, taken immediately after, in place of the footer:
  wall clock end   : 2026-08-12T18:00:00Z UTC (approx; the poll that observed
                     the process gone reported "up 8 days, 9:45")
  load average end : 10.03 11.09 11.08

READ THE loopback RECORD LINE WITH CARE. Its median of 476,571.9us is 5.5x the
tcp median measured in the SAME run and 5.8x the loopback median measured in the
release run twenty minutes earlier. That is a contention artifact, not a
property of the loopback transport: the box was a shared laptop with a
concurrent build competing for the same disk, and the durable flush is the term
that absorbs disk contention. The control series in the same run did NOT move
correspondingly (127.5us), which is precisely why the control is there.

This run is retained as evidence of RUN-TO-RUN VARIANCE in the durability term,
not as a measurement of the loopback transport. The headline figures are in
gate-logs/p0-69/rtt-probe.log.
